    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-221963",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $8a≡8b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ then $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}(\\text{mod}\\,12).\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThen, $8a=$ {{field-8}} and $8b=$ {{field-9}}. \n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{8a}{{field-10}}{{24}_{⏟}}_{8b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{6a}{{field-11}}{{18}_{⏟}}_{6b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-12}} and $b=$ {{field-13}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,8a≡8b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(8a≡8b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12))\\,∧\\,(6a≢6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\n8a≡8b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\,\\text{and}\\,6a≢6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(0,3)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=0,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nThen, $8a=0$ and $8b=24.$ \n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{8a}≡{{24}_{⏟}}_{8b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\,\\text{and}\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{6a}≢{{18}_{⏟}}_{6b}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=0$ and $b=3$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
